The CoSS is most suited to identifying genes with well defined spatial domains of differential expression at scales larger than the measurement resolution. This measure is particularly effective at detecting patterns of exceptionally high or low expression, and for comparing spatial organisation across samples with variable tissue morphology (see boxout). Persistent homology may be less effective at detecting expression gradients without clear boundaries, or detecting patterns in very sparse expression data. We have only tested the current implementation up to a spatial resolution of 10\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
